Java's `ResidualEvaluator` returns `alwaysTrue` for `notEq`, `notIn` and `notNaN` on NULL. [`TestResiduals`](https://github.com/apache/iceberg/blob/acea99d28a4be93a8c0dfc7bd8ece4a237324243/api/src/test/java/org/apache/iceberg/transforms/TestResiduals.java#L251-L290) asserts that "null not in" is true. Your patch also reverses the `NotNaN` expectation from #3689. Are you fine diverging from Java here?

The REST test that you removed covered a different case. Without `residual-filter`, PyIceberg uses `AlwaysTrue`, so `count()` trusts `record_count`. In that test, `count()` returns 3 on main and with your patch, while `to_arrow()` returns 0. The [REST spec](https://github.com/apache/iceberg/blob/acea99d28a4be93a8c0dfc7bd8ece4a237324243/open-api/rest-catalog-open-api.yaml#L5853-L5858) requires the client to compute the residual or use the original filter. Would you like me to fix that here or in a separate PR?
